1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,471
Every change breaks something

2
00:00:02,771 --> 00:00:04,671
and we find out from customers.

3
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:11,197
One line in the policy: require two independent sources, instead of one.

4
00:00:11,497 --> 00:00:14,996
It will stop copies passing as corroboration. Good.

5
00:00:15,296 --> 00:00:21,014
It may also block an answer backed by one authoritative source, like a regulator's notice.

6
00:00:21,314 --> 00:00:23,704
Most teams find out which, after shipping.

7
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:28,843
Replay the change on recorded cases before it ships.

8
00:00:29,143 --> 00:00:35,053
Four results change. Three false admissions are removed: two copies, and a wiki page.

9
00:00:35,353 --> 00:00:40,709
And one correct answer is lost: the regulator's notice, which has a single source.

10
00:00:41,009 --> 00:00:46,108
Now the trade-off is a decision, made before release, not a surprise after it.

11
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:51,826
The console runs the same replay on the Policy changes screen.

12
00:00:52,126 --> 00:00:56,777
Compare results, and every changed verdict is listed, with what it means.

13
00:00:57,077 --> 00:01:02,219
Nothing is applied here. Promoting a policy change is a separate, signed review.

14
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:05,658
Replay first, then decide.

15
00:01:05,958 --> 00:01:16,284
QualityRag shows exactly what a change would fix, and what it would break. Whether that trade is worth it is your decision, and promotion stays a signed review.

16
00:01:16,584 --> 00:01:18,505
That completes the measurement track.
